statement.p:
The ordered triple
\boxed{\bigl(\mathbf T(s),\mathbf N(s),\mathbf B(s)\bigr)}
is called the Frenet frame of the curve.

image.asymptote:
        // 3-D interactive (WebGL) figure.
        import graph3;
        size(11cm);
        currentprojection=orthographic(5,3,2.4);
        currentlight=White;
        defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));

        path3 helix=graph(new triple(real t){return (2*cos(t), 2*sin(t), t);}, 0, 2pi, 220, operator ..);
        draw(helix, blue+linewidth(1.2pt));

        triple P=(-2,0,pi);
        real s5=sqrt(5);
        triple Tdir=(0,-2,1)/s5;
        triple Ndir=(1,0,0);
        triple Bdir=(0,1,2)/s5;

        // Translucent osculating-plane patch through P, spanned by T and N.
        real aN=1.30, aT=1.10;
        triple C1=P-aN*Ndir-aT*Tdir;
        triple C2=P+aN*Ndir-aT*Tdir;
        triple C3=P+aN*Ndir+aT*Tdir;
        triple C4=P-aN*Ndir+aT*Tdir;
        path3 oscplane=C1--C2--C3--C4--cycle;
        draw(surface(oscplane), gray(0.82)+opacity(0.22));
        draw(oscplane, gray(0.55)+linewidth(0.4pt));

        // Frame arrows drawn after the plane so they stay visible.
        real sT=1.55, sN=1.40, sB=1.55;
        draw(P--(P+sT*Tdir), black+linewidth(1.65pt), Arrow3);
        draw(P--(P+sN*Ndir), black+linewidth(1.65pt), Arrow3);
        draw(P--(P+sB*Bdir), black+linewidth(1.65pt), Arrow3);
        label("$\mathbf T$", P+sT*Tdir, SE);
        label("$\mathbf N$", P+sN*Ndir, E);
        label("$\mathbf B$", P+sB*Bdir, N);
        label("osculating plane", C3+(0.15,0,0.15), E);

        dot(P, black+6pt);

        draw(O--(3.4,0,0), gray, Arrow3);
        draw(O--(0,3.4,0), gray, Arrow3);
        draw(O--(0,0,7.0), gray, Arrow3);
        label("$x$",(3.4,0,0),S);
        label("$y$",(0,3.4,0),E);
        label("$z$",(0,0,7.0),N);

statement.p:
Let \mathbf r(s) be a C^3 regular space curve with
\kappa(s)>0. The torsion \tau(s) is defined by
\boxed{\frac{d\mathbf B}{ds}=-\tau\mathbf N.}
Equivalently,
\boxed{\tau=-\frac{d\mathbf B}{ds}\cdot\mathbf N.}

subsection.p:
Since \mathbf N=\mathbf B\times\mathbf T, the product rule for cross products gives
\frac{d\mathbf N}{ds} \amp= \frac{d\mathbf B}{ds}\times\mathbf T + \mathbf B\times\frac{d\mathbf T}{ds}
\amp= (-\tau\mathbf N)\times\mathbf T + \mathbf B\times(\kappa\mathbf N).
Now \mathbf N\times\mathbf T=-\mathbf B and \mathbf B\times\mathbf N=-\mathbf T. Therefore \boxed{\frac{d\mathbf N}{ds}=-\kappa\mathbf T+\tau\mathbf B.}

statement.p:
Let \mathbf r(s) be a C^3 regular curve in \mathbb R^3,
parametrized by arc length, and suppose \kappa(s)>0. Then its Frenet
frame satisfies

  \boxed{
  \begin{aligned}
  \frac{d\mathbf T}{ds} \amp= \kappa\mathbf N,\\
  \frac{d\mathbf N}{ds} \amp= -\kappa\mathbf T+\tau\mathbf B,\\
  \frac{d\mathbf B}{ds} \amp= -\tau\mathbf N.
  \end{aligned}
  }

subsection.p:
The same system can be written in matrix form:

  \boxed{
  \frac{d}{ds}
  \begin{pmatrix}
  \mathbf T\\
  \mathbf N\\
  \mathbf B
  \end{pmatrix}
  =
  \begin{pmatrix}
  0 \amp \kappa \amp 0\\
  -\kappa \amp 0 \amp \tau\\
  0 \amp -\tau \amp 0
  \end{pmatrix}
  \begin{pmatrix}
  \mathbf T\\
  \mathbf N\\
  \mathbf B
  \end{pmatrix}.
  }

The coefficient matrix is skew-symmetric: its transpose is its negative. This is
not an accident. The Frenet frame may rotate, but its vectors remain mutually
perpendicular and keep length 1. A skew-symmetric matrix is exactly the
infinitesimal form of such a rotation.

statement.p:
Let \mathbf r(t) be a C^3 regular curve in \mathbb R^3,
and suppose
\mathbf r'(t)\times\mathbf r''(t)\ne\mathbf 0.
Then

  \boxed{
  \tau(t)
  =
  \frac{
  \bigl(\mathbf r'(t)\times\mathbf r''(t)\bigr)
  \cdot\mathbf r'''(t)}
  {\|\mathbf r'(t)\times\mathbf r''(t)\|^2}.
  }

Equivalently,

  \boxed{
  \tau(t)
  =
  \frac{
  \det\bigl(\mathbf r'(t),\mathbf r''(t),\mathbf r'''(t)\bigr)}
  {\|\mathbf r'(t)\times\mathbf r''(t)\|^2}.
  }

The Frenet frame is

  \boxed{
  \mathbf T(t)
  =
  \frac{1}{\sqrt5}(-2\sin t,2\cos t,1),
  }


  \boxed{
  \mathbf B(t)
  =
  \frac{1}{\sqrt5}(\sin t,-\cos t,2),
  }

and

  \boxed{
  \mathbf N(t)
  =
  \mathbf B(t)\times\mathbf T(t)
  =
  (-\cos t,-\sin t,0).
  }
